it's great to be joined today by Gordon  lifer who's associate professor at the  Labor Education and Research Center at  the University of Oregon he's the author  of the book the one percent solution how  corporations are remaking America one  state at a time such an important issue  Gordon and I think many people in our  audience would of course be familiar  with citizens united and the way that  has impacted money and politics in the  United States but your book makes the  case that there is just so much more  beyond citizens united that is impacting  how corporations are increasingly in  control of different aspects of American  society and government let's start there  I mean what are what are some of the  ways outside of citizens united that  over the last five years we've seen a  move towards corporate power well I mean  the real I think everybody knows  citizens united let there be unlimited  money spent in politics but for one a  lot of I think too much attention has  been paid on individual billionaires  when the real money is with the big  corporations and what this book tries to  say is like so what are they doing now  that they're allowed to spend unlimited  money on politics what are they doing  with that insolence and what they're  doing unfortunately is trying to make  themselves richer and lower the wages  the benefits and the rights on the job  of almost everybody else both Union  non-union public employees private  sector employees they're working to  lower the minimum wage to do away with  the right to paid sick leave to cut  unemployment insurance a lot of things  that you know many people don't pay  attention to until you run into it in  your personal life but the really affect  many many aspects of our economic lives  and before we dig in more specifically  to some of the the instances that you  mentioned what metrics can we look at at  the economy level that reflects what has  been going on over the last five or even  more years along these lines I mean can  we look at certainly you mentioned the  minimum wage what about income and  wealth inequality I mean is that a macro  indicator that tells us how this is  affecting our society or what indicators  can we look at absolutely I mean America  has been becoming a more unequal country  for 40 years and I would say life has  been getting harder  for majority people in the country  slowly but steadily for about 40 years  if you look at the share of all income  that goes to people who work as opposed  to the owners or investors and companies  that's been declining and in 2010 hit  its lowest point ever for men 60% of men  now have seen their wages fall and  inflation adjusted terms over the last  40 years a quarter just to take the  minimum wage as in a long term  one-quarter of all people in the country  now work for less than what the minimum  wage was in 1968 and that means over a  hundred billion dollars a year that is  taken out of the pockets of people who  do the work and given to the people who  own and invest just because of the power  of their politics so there are a lot of  ways to measure it but it's a lot of  money it's a big impact in individuals  lives and it's a big impact for the  country as a whole let's begin - one  aspect of this that you talk about in  the book which is the privatization of  public services and this is one that  correct me if I'm wrong is particularly  noticeable at the state rather than the  federal level that's right I mean  everything that I've studied is at the  state level because Congress is mostly  deadlocked they pass a few big bills a  year but thousands of bills a year get  passed in the states and the states have  power over a lot of our land and the  corporate lobbies have more power in the  states than in the federal government  because nobody's really paying attention  most people are bored by state politics  but a lot of poor working-class and  middle-class families rely on things  like public transportation to get to  work or school or shopping at a fordable  rate Public Health Services from county  and city officials libraries two-thirds  of libraries are the only place in their  communities where you can get free  internet access and of course public  education and all of those things have  been cut drastically and cut not because  there was a budget crisis but because  the corporate lobbies pushed bills to  cut that and some of that is in order to  enable tax cuts for themselves and we've  seen for instance in Ohio and 2011 Ohio  eliminated full-day kindergarten for all  kids in Ohio and at the same year they  eliminated the inheritance tax with all  which only ever affected the 7% richest  families  in the state so we've seen a lot of  things like that taking money away from  public services that people depend on  into the hands of the most privileged  we've been talking about the so-called  tax proposal from Donald Trump which was  really just you know a page of bullet  points that that were lacking a lot of  detail but to the extent that it was a  plan one of the areas where it seems to  me that corporations have been hugely  successful in crafting the narrative  around taxation is the corporate tax  rate which of course is so drastically  different from the effective tax rate  paid by corporations talk a little bit  about that I mean we often hear from  corporations we have one of the highest  tax rates in the world in the u.s. at  thirty five percent corporate tax rates  but that ignores all of the mechanisms  through which the effective tax paid can  sometimes be zero or even negative  that's right I mean America has one of  the highest official kind of formal tax  rates on corporations and one of the  lowest actual effective rates of what  people pay at what corporations pay and  that has meant a couple things one thing  is defunding of public services another  is if you look over time of all of the  taxes that come in to pay for public  services how much is paid by  corporations and how much is paid by  individuals and families the share paid  by individuals has gone up and up and up  as corporations have grown big  departments of accountants whose whole  mission is to minimize how much they  contribute to public services and so you  get people you know individuals who end  up and kind of with anti-tax feelings so  they feel like I'm paying more and more  and getting less and less for it and  they're right their conclusion may not  be right but they're right that that is  what's happening it's happening because  the corporations are getting out of  paying their taxes let's talk a little  bit about populism I mean the 2016  election was interesting in that we had  populist sounding messages from both  Republican and Democratic candidates of  course Bernie Sanders advocating what I  would call progressive populism and  Donald Trump quite the opposite of  regressive populism but you've talked  about how corporate strategy has  actually been at least partially shaped  by the sort of populist sounding  rhetoric of elected officials talk about  that a little bit in what way  we see that well for one thing I think  that you know what does you know we're  in a time now when there's long term  downward a downward decline economic  decline it creates a lot of rage a lot  of insecurity a lot of anxiety and that  makes politics very chaotic and  combustible so you know I think that  we're at the point where the neoliberal  politics of saying well we're going to  have people who are investment bankers  or come from hedge funds but they're a  little bit nice so they care about  something is over I mean that's to say  nobody can ever win an election like  that but we saw both in the Republican  and Democratic parties that life is just  got too hard in America for people to be  satisfied with those kind of candidates  and then that sentiments of wanting  something to change of being angry can  split either right or left what we see  when you look at what the corporate  lobbies are doing corporate lobbies have  a lot of power but what they have failed  to do is to convince most people that  their ideas are good ideas so you see a  majority of both Republicans and  Democrats in favor of raising the  minimum wage in favor of a right to paid  sick leave in favor of fair scheduling  for people who are hourly workers  wanting smaller class size for their  kids and better trained teachers a right  to health insurance a whole lot of  things the corporate lobbies see this  and they're scared by it and I think  they try to do two things one is to get  the anger and anxiety to be channeled in  some direction other than done  themselves whether it's private sector  workers against government workers or  citizens against immigrants or however  you have it but the other is they're  trying to restrict our ability to vote  on things what you see in state after  state where they have the power is  corporate lobbies passing models at the  state level let's say we're going to  take away your right to decide at the  city level whether you want to have a  higher minimum wage or a right to paid  sick leave or something like that that  they're trying to restrict the amount of  the economy that citizens can have  Democratic control over how different is  corporate strategy depending on whether  the context is a left-wing or right-wing  populism I'd be curious to know because  we know that from the political point of  view you can have a populist message  that is a very right-wing message as  we've seen from Donald  which scapegoats the other it presents  him as the only and most direct path to  a solution or improvement to the problem  etc but if we step back from this and  look at it from the corporate  perspective can corporations do really  well whether it's under left or right  wing populism or does it make a  difference I think that they don't do  well under left wing populism and under  right wing populism they're walking down  this path but they're playing with fire  and I think they know it I mean it was  interesting that all of the big  corporate lobbies were opposed to Donald  Trump and basically every other  candidate except him was dependent on  corporate money and did what they wanted  and what they wanted which didn't work  the corporate design for the Tea Party  starting in 2010 was to have a mobilized  conservative base that was only  mobilized around anti-tax and  anti-government and economic Liberty and  get rid of all the social issues which  the money people thought were losers  politically so if you look at the Tea  Party platform a lot of people involved  in the Tea Party may have been  anti-immigrant or anti marriage equality  but there are a class form was only  about economic issues and that's what  the Koch brothers were trying to  engineer with the Tea Party and then  Trump hijacked it and made of both you  know in some ways more more populist on  economic messages and much more  right-wing on anti-immigrant anti-woman  anti-muslim all kinds of things and it's  dangerous and I think the it's dangerous  even for the corporate lobbies but the  more dangerous time I think is going to  come in a few years because Trump  basically ran on the promise of  providing decently paying jobs for  people without college degrees if that  dawns on people that that's not  happening in two or three or four years  his only move is going to be to double  down even more on whipping up white  nationalist frenzy I think by that time  the progressive populist need to have  organized enough of the people who put  faith in Trump for for helping  working-class people to move on to a  more progressive platform we've been  talking about the book the one percent  solution how corporations are remaking  America one state at a time and we've  been speaking with the book's author  Gordon Laver who's associate professor  at the Labor Education and Research  Center at the University of Oregon  Gordon thanks so much for talking to us  about  thanks very much for having me

